
Changing your sanitary pad regularly is one of the simplest ways to maintain good menstrual hygiene. However, there is no exact number of hours that applies to everyone because menstrual flow differs from person to person and can also change from one day to another.

Instead of following a strict timetable, it is better to check your pad regularly and change it whenever necessary.

How Often Should You Change It?

Many people change their sanitary pad every few hours while they are actively bleeding. However, the exact timing depends on your flow, the absorbency of the pad, your activity level, and your personal comfort.

If you have heavy flow, your pad may become saturated quickly and require more frequent changes.

On lighter days, the pad may not fill as quickly. Even so, you should avoid wearing the same pad for an unnecessarily long period.

Signs That It Is Time to Change

You should change your pad when:

It feels wet or uncomfortable
It is heavily saturated
Menstrual fluid is approaching the edges
You notice leakage
The pad has shifted or become uncomfortable
You simply feel that it needs to be replaced

You do not need to wait until the pad is completely full.

Proper Pad-Changing Hygiene

Before changing your pad, wash your hands thoroughly. Remove the used pad carefully and wrap it securely before placing it in a suitable waste bin.

Never flush a sanitary pad down the toilet because it can cause plumbing problems.

After changing, wash your hands again.

The external genital area can be gently cleaned with water. Avoid excessive scrubbing or strongly fragranced products if they cause irritation.

Choose the Right Absorbency

The pad you choose can affect how often you need to change it.

If your flow is heavy, a light or regular pad may become saturated quickly. Choosing a larger, higher-absorbency product can provide more protection.

